  "title": "Erdős Problem #1055",
  "statement": {
    "representation": "markdown-latex",
    "text": "A prime $p$ is in class $1$ if the only prime divisors of $p+1$ are $2$ or $3$. In general, a prime $p$ is in class $r$ if every prime factor of $p+1$ is in some class $\\leq r-1$, with equality for at least one prime factor.\nAre there infinitely many primes in each class? If $p_r$ is the least prime in class $r$, then how does $p_r^{1/r}$ behave?",
